\DOC LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually} goal about machine state {sn} across one subroutine
call by instantiating a named universally quantified "safety" specification
assumption, replacing the pre-call state assumptions with post-call ones for
state {s(n+1)}.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {assump_name : string} labels the quantified specification
assumption in the goal's assumption list that is used to step over the call;
the second, {n : int}, is the index of the current state, so the conclusion's
state variable {sn} becomes {s(n+1)} and the new assumptions are stated about
{s(n+1)}.
{
      A ?- eventually ... sn
   ========================================  LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n
      A' ?- eventually ... s(n+1)
}
\noindent The conclusion is unchanged apart from the state variable. In the
assumptions, all facts about the old state {sn} (register and memory reads,
flag facts, {bytes_loaded}, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame, and the
event-trace equation) are discharged and replaced by corresponding facts about
{s(n+1)}: a new program-counter and stack-pointer value, a rewritten {read
events s(n+1) = APPEND ...} equation mentioning a freshly introduced trace
variable {z}, two new assumptions characterising {z} (an equation defining it
via the callee's event function and a {memaccess_inbounds} fact about it), the
surviving loaded-code and memory reads, and an extended {MAYCHANGE} frame
covering the callee's writes. The named assumption and the other quantified
specification assumption are retained, with the named one moved to the end of
the list, and non-state side conditions such as the {nonoverlapping} hypotheses
are kept.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal transformation, always on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement and whose assumptions
include the named quantified specification.

\ENDDOC
